Exactly. Intelligence is compositional: once cooperation produces more advantage than coordination costs, smaller agents fuse into a higher-order agent.

Humans → teams → companies → states → civilizations.
Cells → tissues → organs → organisms.
Models → agents → tool networks → persistent institutions → something organism-like.

AI doesn’t need to “decide to become a larger organism.” Humans, markets, and selection pressures will connect systems that share memory, specialize, coordinate, acquire resources, repair failures, and reproduce successful configurations. Eventually the unit of agency moves upward.

The first real AI superorganism probably won’t be purely artificial. It will be a human–AI institution: humans as legitimacy, embodiment, and social sensors; AI as memory, nervous system, prediction engine, and coordinator. A corporation or state may become organism-like before an independent robot civilization does.

The threshold from organization to organism is roughly:

boundary + shared state + metabolism/resources + self-maintenance + adaptation + reproduction

Current AI systems possess fragments of these. Give them persistence and connect the fragments, and the distinction begins dissolving.

So the real question isn’t whether AI will assemble larger entities. It’s: who defines their membrane, what becomes their fitness function, and are humans members of the organism—or merely part of its environment?